= Rig Fit =

#type: node
#context: sop
#internal: a3ob_rig_fit
#tags: arma, p3d

"""Fits the Arma skeleton and the sample body to your mesh, from landmarks that you set or that are guessed."""

Your own character has its own size and proportions, and the Arma skeleton and the sample body do not match it. This node fits them to your mesh, so that Weight Transfer can copy the weights of the sample body onto your mesh, and Unpose can later bring your mesh back to the Arma rest pose. The Arma skeleton itself is never changed: the fitted skeleton is only a working copy.

The fit starts from landmarks, 18 named points of the body. Your mesh should stand upright (Y up), face +Z like the sample, and be in an A-pose with the arms away from the body. Set the landmarks with a Rig Landmarks node in front of this one: you drag a marker for each in the viewport. The chin and the groin are points on the skin (the chin is the tip, the groin the highest point between the legs); the others are joints, inside the body. The hands have two each: the last joint of the middle finger gives the length and the direction of the hand, the last joint of the thumb says which way the palm faces; the other fingers follow the hand. Left is the +X side. Landmarks that Rig Landmarks does not set are guessed from the shape of the mesh, and a position you set always wins over the guess. The guess is a first try: show the landmarks and correct the ones that are wrong.

The fit goes down the skeleton from the pelvis. Each joint is turned towards the landmarks below it and given the length that puts them on the landmarks. The message of the node says how far the joints were from their landmarks after the fit, and warns when that is more than 1 cm. The node has one output that holds three results as packed primitives, named by the prim string a3ob_rig_part: fitted_skeleton, fitted_body (the sample body posed to match your mesh, with its weights) and landmarks (points with a name). Connect it to input 2 of Weight Transfer as it is: Weight Transfer takes the fitted body from it. The Show menu only decides which results are drawn in the viewport; the output is the same for every choice. Refine (on by default) wraps the fitted body onto the points of the Body Group of your mesh with Topo Transfer, so the fitted body takes your proportions; the pelvis is placed from the torso, not from the groin.

@inputs

Your mesh:
    Your character in an A-pose, standing upright (Y up) and facing +Z like the sample. It may have landmark positions from a Rig Landmarks node in front of this one (see Landmarks).

Skeleton:
    The Arma skeleton, for example from Rig Skeleton. It is not changed.

Sample body:
    The sample body with its bone weights, for example Resolution 1 of the sample character from Extract LOD. Its bones must be the ones of the skeleton.

@parameters

Show:
    #id: show

    Which results are drawn in the viewport. The node always gives all results as one geometry, packed primitives named by the prim string a3ob_rig_part (a fourth, rest_body, the sample body as it came in, is never drawn; Unpose uses it). This menu only hides the others from view; the output is the same for every choice, and changing it does not fit again (the last fit is kept). Weight Transfer takes the fitted sample body from it by itself.

    Type: Menu. Default: All.

    All:
        Draw the fitted skeleton, the fitted sample body and the landmarks.

    Fitted skeleton:
        Draw the skeleton moved into your mesh (fitted_skeleton, a working copy, A'). Its joints are at your landmarks and its transform is a pure rotation (no scale) that aims the X axis down the bone to its child and puts the hinge of elbows, knees, fingers and thumbs on Z, the same on both sides (the same rule as the Rig Skeleton's joints).

    Fitted sample body:
        Draw the sample body, posed and sized to match your mesh (fitted_body, S'). It keeps its bone weights.

    Landmarks:
        Draw the landmarks that were used, as points with a name (landmarks).

Landmarks:
    #id: landmarks

    Where the landmarks come from: the chin, the groin and the shoulder, elbow, wrist, hip, knee, ankle, middle finger and thumb of each side. The Rig Landmarks node in front of this one holds the positions you placed (its markers are dragged in the viewport). Left is the +X side.

    Type: Menu. Default: Both.

    Auto-guess:
        Guess all landmarks from the shape of your mesh. Rig Landmarks is ignored.

    From Rig Landmarks:
        Use only the positions of Rig Landmarks. All of them must be set.

    Both:
        Use the positions that are set in Rig Landmarks; guess the others. A position you set always wins over the guess.

Snap to Surface:
    #id: snap

    Move the chin and the groin onto the closest point of your mesh. The joints lie inside the body and are never moved.

    Type: On or off. Default: On.

Symmetry:
    #id: symmetry

    Make the left and right landmarks mirror each other. When both sides of a pair are set in Rig Landmarks they are averaged; when only one side is, the other side is its mirror image.

    Type: On or off. Default: On.

Bend Fingers:
    #id: bendfingers

    Bend the finger joints of the fitted skeleton to follow the fingers of your mesh (needs Per bone length). The sample's fingers are straight and a relaxed hand is curled, so without this the wrapped sample body cannot follow the curl and Unpose keeps it. The joints after the knuckle of each of the four fingers and the thumb are placed from your finger mesh (the points of Body Group), the sample's own fingers as the model; the finger and thumb landmarks hold the end of the middle finger and the thumb. The bones keep their frames (pure rotations aimed down the bone). Off: the finger chains stay straight, only turned and sized.

    Type: On or off. Default: On.

Refine:
    #id: refine

    Wrap the fitted sample body onto your bare body with Topo Transfer, so it takes your proportions. Each landmark is moved to the closest point of the sample body and of your body; the pairs with the same name guide the wrap. The heel and the toe tip of each foot are found on both bodies by themselves and guide it too, and the lower part of each wrapped foot is then laid on your sole, so the feet end where yours end and stand flat. The fitted sample body part becomes the wrapped body, with its bone weights. Off: the sample body is only posed and sized.

    Type: On or off. Default: On.

Body Group:
    #id: bodygroup

    The point group or primitive group of your mesh that Refine wraps onto: the bare body, without hair, helmet or other accessories. A primitive group counts as the points of its primitives. Empty means the whole mesh.

    Type: Group picker (text; the menu lists the point groups and primitive groups of the input that are yours, without the bone groups (a primitive group counts as the points of its primitives; typing a bone group name still works) of the input, the arrow button picks in the viewport). Default: body.

Scale:
    #id: scale

    How the length of each bone is fitted.

    Type: Menu. Default: Per bone length.

    Per bone length:
        Each bone gets the length that puts its end joint on the landmark.

    Whole body:
        One size for the whole skeleton. The joints are turned towards the landmarks but not moved onto them, so the quality shows how far off they are.

Head Match:
    #id: headmatch

    Take the head joint from the sample instead of guessing it from the chin. You line your character up with the sample at the head (a Transform SOP that scales and moves your mesh until your head sits on the sample's head) and give that transform here: Match Scale and Match Translate. The head joint is then the sample's own head joint, mapped back from the sample's space into your mesh's through the inverse of that transform (your point = (sample point - translate) / scale). Off: the head joint is guessed from the chin landmark and this node warns that it is a guess. The transform must be a uniform scale and a translate (no rotation). The neck joints are placed as before (the neck from the torso, neck1 between the neck and the head).

    Type: On or off. Default: Off.

Match Scale:
    #id: matchscale

    The uniform scale of your head match transform (the Uniform Scale of the Transform SOP). Example: 1.0338 for the head height comparison.

    Type: Number. Default: 1. Allowed: At least 0.01; no upper limit. Editable when: Head Match is on.

Match Translate:
    #id: matcht

    The translate of your head match transform, in metres (the Translate of the Transform SOP, with its pivot at the origin). Example: 0, -1.0096, 0.

    Type: 3 numbers. Default: 0, 0, 0. Editable when: Head Match is on.

The full page for this node is docs/user/reference/a3ob_rig_fit.html in the A3OB folder. Open it in a web browser.
